<article class="env-lemma" id="034Q">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/034Q">Lemma <span data-tag="034Q">10.120.2</span>.</a> Let $R$ be a domain. Let $x, y \in R$. Then $x$, $y$ are associates if and only if $(x) = (y)$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      If $x = uy$ for some unit $u \in R$, then $(x) \subset (y)$ and $y = u^{-1}x$ so also $(y) \subset (x)$. Conversely, suppose that $(x) = (y)$. Then $x = fy$ and $y = gx$ for some $f, g \in A$. Then $x = fg x$ and since $R$ is a domain $fg = 1$. Thus $x$ and $y$ are associates. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>